e you a three-dimensional mesh  there's no way in the built-in function  to say I want to have a two ish  dimensional surface embedded in 3-space  so what I did was I flattened the data  by eliminating the Z coordinate make the  mesh using two aligning mesh extract the  mesh lines using mesh primitives and  then restoring the Z coordinate and in  2d that was pretty good and to make a  new point this is essentially I copied  from the code from the 1980s because I  figured why not segment line you find  the Euclidean distance between the x and  y coordinates pick a random number that  in the range that depends on how big the  segment is and then apply some little  sugar to make it smoother or flatter  bumpier so as it turned out if I use the  including distance between the two  actual points and not the points  projected on the plane the whole thing  blew up so the function that's the  a new point is sensitive to what the  conditions are in this plays out later  so here's the function it takes a list  of data which are the previous set of  triples and returns a new data set which  has the previous set plus the new ones  so the comments work pretty well you  take the Delaney mesh by dropping the  z-coordinate which is the last one get  the mesh lines by taking the mesh  primitives makes the lines from that and  then generate the new point and then add  them together everything's hunky-dory  and I'm not gonna execute any of this  code we can make this a little bigger  but here's a sample grid and that looks  pretty good let's see if we can make  that here I did not try to make this any  fancier than necessary because all I  wanted to do was to see whether the  basic code worked okay  for the planet going to the same thing  except now we need to use spherical  coordinates and I had to look up this is  a copied right from the documentation  because as pointed out in math world  there's no standard way of notating  spherical coordinates some of them have  the r-value first